WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:09.500
Marcus: Morning all. Quick round. Aisha, you start?

2
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:27.000
Aisha: Sure. Yesterday I finished the Postgres 16 upgrade on staging. Everything passed, so I'd like to do production on Saturday night when traffic is lowest.

3
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:36.000
Marcus: Saturday works. Make sure we have a fresh snapshot before you start.

4
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:45.000
Aisha: Already scheduled. I'll take the snapshot at 10pm and start the upgrade at 11.

5
00:00:46.000 --> 00:01:05.000
Tom: My update: the CI pipeline is flaky again. About one in five runs fails on the integration suite, and rerunning fixes it. It's the tests that hit the fake payment server.

6
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:18.000
Marcus: That's been going on for weeks. Do we know the cause?

7
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:38.000
Tom: I think the fake server starts slower than the tests expect. There's a hard-coded two second sleep. I want to replace it with a proper health check wait.

8
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:47.000
Marcus: Do it. Tom owns fixing the flaky integration tests this sprint.

9
00:02:40.000 --> 00:03:02.000
Aisha: One more thing. We had an incident on Tuesday night. The payment webhook endpoint timed out for about twenty minutes and we lost some events from Stripe.

10
00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:15.000
Marcus: Yeah, that's the same issue blocking the launch. I'm adding retries with exponential backoff.

11
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:31.000
Aisha: Can we also add an alert? Nobody noticed until a customer emailed support the next morning.

12
00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:44.000
Marcus: Good call. Let's add a pager alert when webhook failures go above one percent. Aisha, can you set that up?

13
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:50.000
Aisha: Yes, I'll have the alert in place by Thursday.
